tcrm
----

tcrm is a self-hosted CRM and customer-service stack: the Relaticle CRM,
Chatwoot for support chat, Evolution API for WhatsApp and n8n for
automations, behind a Traefik reverse proxy.  It runs with Docker
Compose on one host, over a shared PostgreSQL (with pgvector) where
every application has its own database and user, plus Redis.  The CRM
exposes an MCP endpoint, so AI agents can work with its data.

Setup Guide
----- -----
This guide outlines the manual steps required to bootstrap the TCRM
Docker environment from scratch.

Prerequisites
-------------
Ensure you have the following installed on your host machine:
- Docker and Docker Compose
- openssl (for generating secure keys)
- htpasswd/apache2-utils (for Traefik dashboard basic auth)

Docker Permissions
------ -----------
If this is your first time running Docker on this machine, you may
encounter a "permission denied while trying to connect to the docker API
at unix:///var/run/docker.sock" error.

To fix this so you do not have to use `sudo` for every Docker command,
add your current user to the docker group:

1. Add the user:
   `sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`

2. Apply the new group:
   `newgrp docker`

Step 1: Generate Secrets
---- -- -------- -------
Before booting the stack, you must generate secure keys and hashes for
the environment variables.  Run the following commands in your terminal
and save the outputs:

1. Traefik Dashboard Auth Hash:
   `htpasswd -nbB <your_admin_username> <your_password>`

2. Relaticle App Key (Base64 32-byte string):
   `echo "base64:$(openssl rand -base64 32)"`

3. Chatwoot Secret Key (Hex 64-byte string):
   `openssl rand -hex 64`

4. Evolution API Master Key:
   `openssl rand -hex 32`

5. n8n Encryption Key (for n8n's credential vault):
   `openssl rand -base64 32`

Step 2: Environment Configuration
---- -- ----------- -------------
1. Copy the template file:
   `cp .env.example .env`

2. Open `.env` and fill in all the blank variables:
   - Paste the Traefik hash into DASHBOARD_USERS.
   - Paste the Relaticle key into RELATICLE_APP_KEY.
   - Paste the Chatwoot key into CHATWOOT_SECRET_KEY.
   - Paste the Evolution key into EVOLUTION_API_KEY.
   - Paste the n8n key into N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY.
   - Set secure passwords for POSTGRES_PASSWORD and REDIS_PASSWORD.
   - Set passwords for all application database users.

Step 3: Local DNS Routing (Optional but recommended)
---- -- ----- --- ------- --------- --- ------------
If your system or browser does not automatically resolve `*.localhost`
to your local machine, you need to map them manually.

1. Open your hosts file (requires root):
   `sudo ed /etc/hosts`

2. Add the following line:
   `127.0.0.1  dashboard.localhost crm.localhost chat.localhost evolution.localhost n8n.localhost`

Step 4: First-time Provisioning
---- -- ---------- ------------
Because of dependency requirements and strict database security, you
cannot boot the entire stack blindly the very first time.  Chatwoot
requires its database to be manually prepared and relies on several
PostgreSQL extensions that its restricted user cannot create.

1. Boot ONLY the Tier 1 database layer first to allow Postgres to
   initialize the schemas:
   `docker compose up -d postgres redis`

2. Wait 60 to 120 seconds.  Verify Postgres is ready:
   `docker compose ps`
   (Wait until the postgres container shows as "healthy").

3. Inject required database extensions:
   By default, the chatwoot database user is restricted.  You must log
   in as the Postgres superuser to install all 5 extensions Chatwoot
   needs.  Run this exact command:
   `docker exec -i tcrm-postgres-1 psql -U postgres -d chatwoot_db -f -
    <<'SQL'
     CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgcrypto;
     CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;
     CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gin;
     CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_stat_statements;
     CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;
   SQL`

4. Prepare the chatwoot database:
   Run the one-off database preparation command.  This will create the
   tables and seed the initial data:
   `docker compose run --rm chatwoot bundle exec rake db:chatwoot_prepare`

Step 5: Boot The Full Stack
---- -- ---- --- ---- -----
Once the Chatwoot migration finishes successfully, you can bring up the
rest of the proxy and application layer:

1. Boot everything in the background:
   `docker compose up -d`

2. Verify all services report as "Up" and "healthy":
   `docker compose ps`

Unlike Chatwoot, Evolution API and n8n handle their own database
migrations on startup.  Evolution API takes about 15-30 seconds to run
its Prisma migrations.  You can check its progress by running:
   `docker compose logs --tail=50 evolution`
   (Wait until you see "HTTP - ON: 8080").

Step 6: Initial Configuration & MCP Verification
---- -- ------- ------------- - --- ------------
Once the entire stack is online, you must create your master CRM account
and verify the AI integration endpoint.

1. Create the Master Account:
   Open your browser and navigate to http://crm.localhost
   Click "Log In" or "Get Started" to access the registration page.
   The first user created in a fresh database is automatically granted
   Superadmin privileges and creates the default Workspace.

2. Generate an AI API Token:
   Inside the Relaticle dashboard, click your user profile.
   Navigate to the API Tokens/Personal Access Tokens section.
   Create a new token (e.g., "MCP Testing") and grant it ALL permissions
   (Create, Read, Update, Delete).
   Copy the generated token immediately.  It will not be shown again.

3. Verify the MCP (Model Context Protocol) Endpoint:
   The MCP server requires a strict JSON-RPC POST request to communicate.
   Run this command in your terminal to ask the CRM for a list of tools,
   replacing <YOUR_TOKEN_HERE> with your copied token:

   curl -i -X POST \
     -H "Accept: application/json" \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer <YOUR_TOKEN_HERE>" \
     -d '{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 1, "method": "tools/list", "params": {}}' \
     http://crm.localhost/mcp

   If successful, you will receive an `HTTP/1.1 200 OK` response
   containing a JSON array of all available CRM tools (e.g.,
   create_company, list_people, etc.).  The MCP endpoint is then ready
   for AI agents.

Accessing The Application
--------- --- -----------
Traefik Dashboard : http://dashboard.localhost (Uses htpasswd credentials)
Relaticle CRM     : http://crm.localhost
Chatwoot          : http://chat.localhost
Evolution API     : http://evolution.localhost
N8N               : http://n8n.localhost

PostgreSQL and Redis are published on 127.0.0.1 only, for local
administration.  The applications reach them over the internal Docker
network.

To shut down the environment safely without losing data:
`docker compose down`

To completely wipe the environment AND all database data:
`docker compose down -v`

DO NOT use the `-v` flag unless your explicit goal is to completely
destroy the environment.  If you run this command, you will instantly
lose all Relaticle configurations, all Chatwoot messages, the underlying
database schemas, and the custom PostgreSQL extensions.

If you accidentally run this, you must repeat Step 4 to rebuild the
databases from scratch.

